Shores of Hazeron
-----------------
	The multiplayer sandbox universe.

	Shores of Hazeron is a system of programs that manages a model of a
	Universe for multiplayer play.

	The Universe is a hierarchical data structure containing DaAtom derived 
	objects, referred to simply as 'atoms'. Atoms store a position and a 
	rotation relative to their parent atom.

	At the root of the atom tree is the one and only DaUniverse atom.

	Twentyone DaGalaxy atoms exist subordinate to the DaUniverse atom. No other
	kinds of atoms are subordinated to a DaUniverse.

	DaSector atoms exist subordinate to each DaGalaxy atom. No other kinds of
	atoms are subordinated to a DaGalaxy.

	DaSolarSystem atoms exist subordinate to DaSector atoms. No other kinds of
	atoms are subordinated to a DaSector.

	All remaining atom classes exist subordinate to a DaSolarSystem or an atom
	contained within a DaSolarSystem. For example, a DaSc spacecraft atom can
	exist subordinate to the DaSolarSystem, a DaStar, a DaWorld, or even
	another DaSpaceShip.

	Multiple worker servers manage separate parts of the universe so the
	entire universe can be spread across many servers.

	Client programs never receive the entire universe because it is expected to
	be too big for their computer's limited resources. Instead a scene is
	developed at an avatar's location. Only the atoms that are needed to create
	the scene are sent to the client.

	Client program presents the scene that it has been given.

	Servers create solar system contents only when needed. Solar systems are
	allowed to return to an empty state when not needed. All of this is to keep
	server load to a minimum.

	Sectors are created only when needed. They are allowed to go away when no
	longer needed.

System Requirements
-------------------
	Here is what is currently required if you want to build and run the source
	code "as is". Of course, this is not cast in concrete; determined
	programmers can change anything, given access to the source code. "As is"
	probably gets you up and running faster.

	The server farm is a hierarchy of servers. There is one single scene master
	server. There is one or more scene manager servers, one at each geographic
	location where one or more scene worker servers are located.

	The scene manager creates new solar systems and it delegates solar systems
	to scene worker servers to process. Scene workers report load information
	to their manager. The manager balances the load that is distributed across
	its workers, revoking solar systems from heavily loaded workers and
	assigning them to lightly loaded servers.

	Clients connect to the scene master. From there they are routed to the
	scene worker server hosting their avatar. This hand off happens via the
	scene manager of that particular scene worker server; the scene master
	never speaks directly with a scene worker. The scene worker serves the
	current scene visible to the avatar.

	The server farm requires a minimum of two Linux boxes to host the scene
	master and scene manager servers. They cannot be at the same IP address; I
	don't really remember why. Each server box can run as many scene worker
	servers as they can support. Additional boxes can host more scene worker
	servers, as many as they can support. Memory and CPU threads are the
	limiting factors.

	A PostgreSQL server is also needed. It can be on one of the server boxes
	but it will add to the CPU load considerably. I always used a separate
	Linux box for the SQL server and it stayed pretty busy with eight CPU cores.

	Login and mail servers can coexist on any of the server boxes. They are
	pretty trivial and run as either a master or a worker. The master decides
	which worker to send someone to and hands them off. The worker does the SQL
	queries and responds to the client.

	Each SoH server box requires a static IP address, unless you are setting the
	whole thing up on a LAN. The SQL server does not require a static IP address
	unless any of the SoH servers is remotely located; no client program
	accesses it.

	In the original server farm, the SQL server was on a LAN with the SoH
	servers. The SoH servers had a second network port through which they
	connected directly to the Internet. The SQL server did not have access to
	the Internet for security reasons. As a build machine it had source code
	that I didn't want to risk getting hacked.

	A minimal server farm might look like this:
		u1 - Unit 1
			PostgreSQL database server.
			On my server farm, this box was also the build machine for the
			servers. They had shell scripts to get their updates from here.
		u2 - Unit 2
			login master server
			mail master server
			scene master server
			scene worker server
		u3 - Unit 3
			login worker server
			mail worker server
			scene manager server
			scene worker server

	Additional servers might look like any or all of these:
		u4 - Unit 4
			login worker server
			mail worker server
			scene worker server
		u5 - Unit 5
			scene worker server
		u6 - Unit 6
			scene worker server 1
			scene worker server 2
		u7 - Unit 7
			scene worker server 1
			scene worker server 2
			scene worker server 3
			scene worker server 4

	The servers have never been built for Windows.

	The client can be built for Linux or Windows. I always used Linux because I
	prefer the development environment; 'vi' and 'gdb', what more do you need.
	The software would certainly benefit a lot if the client-side development
	was done using Windows.

	The Windows build is currently performed at the command line with a bat
	file. I don't use Visual Studio so there are no up-to-date project files.
	A programmer familiar with Visual Studio could recreate them. It was done
	once, long ago.

	The code base was started in 1999. It has undergone many significant
	evolutionary changes along the way. That's my way of apologizing for the
	parts of the code that are particularly ugly or difficult to understand.

Makefiles
---------
	Most Makefiles are automatically generated by qmake so they are not stored
	in source control. Their generation is controlled using reqt.opt files.
	Look at the reqt script for more information. On Windows, this is done
	using some ugly .BAT files: REQT.BAT and REQTR.BAT.

	There were a large number of build warnings. Something must have been
	updated or deprecated because there weren't so many of them a few
	years ago when I last built this project.

	I normally clean up all build warnings except those reminding me of
	unfinished bits of code. Unfinished loose ends are often marked with a DHH
	tag.

	Compilers on different platforms generate warnings that do not appear on all
	platforms. It pays to look at every single warning with a hairy eyeball. The
	compiler is not always right.

Developing on Linux
-------------------
	Prerequisites
		qtbase5-dev
		qt5-qmake
		libopenal-dev
		libsdl2-dev
		cmake      			I'm not sure if this one is needed.
	
	Source code in my development folder.
		~/dev/au
	
	Platform file should be copied to the parent folder of my development
	folder and configured accordingly. This way getting fresh source won't
	break it.
		cp linuxPlatform.h ~/dev

	Debug build
		cd ~/dev/au
		make reqt				Updates/recreates makefiles for debug build.
		make					Builds all projects
	
	Release build
		cd ~/dev/au
		make -f Makefile.rel reqt	Updates/recreates makefiles for release
		make -f Makefile.rel        build.

Developing on Windows
---------------------
	Prerequisites
		Environment variables can be configured in your user settings.
			SEiDEV=c:\dev			The folder that contains the project.
			AUARCH=soh-win64		Can be soh-win64 or soh-win32.
		Packages
			OpenAL11CoreSDK.exe installed to c:\dev\OpenAL 1.1 SDK
			Copied folder SDL2-2.0.3 from archive SDL2-2.0.3.zip to c:\dev\
			Qt 5.7.0 installed with default settings.

	Source code in my development folder.
		C:\dev\au

	Build all projects in release configuration that are needed to run the
	client programs. Servers are not built on Windows. A command prompt is used.
		c:
		cd \dev\au
		Build-Au-Source.bat

Files and Folders
-----------------
	GD - Game data file. Contains the a_Item table for the client program 
		because the client cannot talk to the SQL server.
	GS - Solo game data. Contains designs of buildings, ships, and items. Also 
		contains initial empire data.
	linuxPlatform.h - Indicates Linux build platform. See notes in the file.
	motds - Messages of the day delivered by the scene master server. Must be 
		copied to the folder from which the scene master server is run.

	Makefile - gdb makefile for Linux debug build.
	Makefile.rel - gdb makefile for Linux release build.

	auenv.bat - Called by nmakeall.bat to configure environment variables.
	nmakeall.bat - Windows script to build at the command line. Should use 
		command line configured for your development environment.
	nmakeallclean.bat - Cleans all subprojects of build files.

	Blueprints - Folder contains subfolders of .SoH files that are used in the
		game.

	auacctemail - CGI program to change the email address of a login account.
	auacctexpire - Command line program run daily to expire login accounts 
		whose subscription has expired.
	auacctnew - CGI program to create a new login account.
	auacctpassword - CGI program to change the password of a login account.
	auacctsendpassword - CGI program to email a new randomly generated password 
		to a login account's email address.

	auatom - Atom classes and classes used by atoms.
	    auether.h - This is the container class for "live" atoms.
		auatommanager.h - Manages tick and tock threads to animate the atoms 
			of the universe.
		augame - Base class for games played at a game table.
		austory - Manages/compiles stories and story instances for avatars.
		dttick.h - This thread provides high frequence CPU access to atoms, 
			mostly for time critical things like physics movement.
		dttock.h - This thread provides low frequency CPU access to atoms. 
			Cities create their reports in this thread.
		da* - Atom classes derived from DaAtom.
		dp* - Classes used by atoms but not atoms themselves. Particles(?)

	auatomres - Resources needed by atoms. These are broken into multiple 
		projects because the Windows compiler chokes when they get too big.
		When building on Windows, a project that gets too big causes a stack 
		overflow error or out of memory error. To fix the problem, move
		resources into smaller projects and they will build ok. Add more 
		projects if needed.
	auatomr1
	auatomr2   
	auatomr3
	auatomr4
	auatomr5
	auatomr6

	aubodypartsres - Resources for the bodies of old style "alien" DNA.
	aubodyparts0
	aubodyparts1
	aubodyparts2
	aubodyparts3
	aubodyparts4
	aubodyparts5
	aubodyparts6
	aubodyparts7
	aupowerarmor - Power armor body parts for old style "alien" DNA.

	auchatclient - Client GUI program for chatting and doing whatever you can 
		without creating an OpenGL scene. OpenGL is not initialized at all.

	auclient - Library of classes that are used by the client but not by the 
		servers.

	auclientmailres - Resources that must be accessible by the mail client 
		program in addition to the scene client program. These include the 
		little icons that appear in the mail messages such as city reports and 
		diplomatic messages.

	auclientres - Resources needed by the scene client program but not needed 
		by the mail client program.
	auclientres1

	audata - Library of classes that do not depend on DaAtom.

	audesigner - Library of classes used by the building/spacecraft/item 
		designer.

	auempirereport - Command line program that started life as the tool for 
		creating the empire report web pages for the web site empire standings 
		and avatar pages. It evolved into a general purpose utility for 
		interacting with the databases at the command line.

	augl - Library of classes for rendering in OpenGL and loading 3D models.
		glsl - Folder contains GL Shader Language source code. This is
			compressed into glsl.har, which is stored in source control so
			Windows doesn't need hashpack.

			GLSL source code is compressed into an archive so it is not
			bound to the executable as clear text. This was to prevent a
			simple 'strings' command from extracting it.

			If changes are made to any shaders, the glsl.har file must be
			recreated as follows.

				cd augl/glsl
				hashpack -c ../glsl.har *.glsl

	auglobal - Library of classes with few dependencies.
		sfx - Folder contains sound effects WAV files. These are normally
			stored in a file called sfx.har. That file is too big for GitHub
			but it is easy to recreate using hashpack.

				cd auglobal/sfx
				hashpack -c sfx.har *.wav

			WAV files are compressed into an archive so they can be distributed
			without the end user having direct access to the files, most of
			which were purchased from SoundDogs.com.

		auatomticktock - Atom manager classes for managing DtTick and DtTock
			threads. 
		ausound - Class for loading and playing sound effects.
		ps* - Files contain classes derived from AuParticleSystem for rendering
			various visual effects.

	aulauncher - Client GUI program that downloads and runs the release client.

	auloginclient - Library of classes for interacting with the login server.

	auloginserver - Server program for processing login requests from clients, 
		servers, and the web site. Runs as master or worker.
		Launch options:
		-nNetNameOrIPAddr - DNS resolvable name or ip address of this computer.
		-master - Says this computer is the login master.

	aumail - Library of classes for handling Hazeron mail.

	aumailclient - Client GUI program for getting and sending Hazeron mail.

	aumailserver - Server program for processing Hazeron mail. Runs as master 
		or worker.
		Launch options:
			-nNetNameOrIPAddr - DNS resolvable name or ip address of this computer.
			-master - Says this computer is the mail master.

	aumath - Library of classes for math and geometry.

	aumodelres - Resource library of 3D model data.
	aumodel1
	aumodel2
	aumodel3
	aumodel4
	aumodel5
	aumodel6
	aumodel7
	aumodel8
	aumodel9
	aumodel10

	ausceneclient - Client GUI program for playing Shores of Hazeron.

	ausceneserver - Server program for hosting the Hazeron universe online. 
		Runs as master, manager, or worker, depending on settings.
			master.ini - Sample config file for the scene master server.
			manager.ini - Sample config file for a scene manager server.
			worker.ini - Sample config file for a scene worker server.

		Settings file is indicated on the command line when launching a
		scene server.

		In server folder on server computer.
			./godebug =master &		Launches server using master.ini config.
			./godebug =manager &	Launches server using manager.ini config.
			./godebug =worker &		Launches server using worker.ini config.

		Using multiple settings files enables multiple workers to run on
		the same computer, as long as their ports don't overlap. This is
		useful to limit each worker to a specific number of cores, rather than
		running one single monolithic server controlling all CPU cores. Fewer
		threads per process makes it a little easier to backtrace lockups using
		gdb.
			./godebug =worker1 &	Launches server using worker1.ini config.
			./godebug =worker2 &	Launches server using worker2.ini config.
			./godebug =worker3 &	Launches server using worker3.ini config.
			./godebug =worker4 &	Launches server using worker4.ini config.

	auscript - Command line program to compile story scripts.

	autexture - Resource library of texture images.
	ausctex1
	ausctex2
	ausctex3
	ausctex4
	ausctex5
	aushiptex0

	auservermon - Client GUI program for monitoring and managing online 
		servers. Slowly replacing the utility functions of auempirereport.

	auserverstatus - Command line program that is run on the web server to 
		query the scene master server for status.

	austoryres - Library containing all storyline scripts and associated 
		resources.

	auutil - Library of classes with no(?) outside dependencies.
		auversion.h - Contains current version of the software. See comments 
			in the file.
		aufilever.h - Contains version number and magic number for every kind 
			of file that gets serialized.

	auviewer - Stand alone GUI program for viewing DNA and other things?

	hashpack - Utility for creating a compressed archive. This has never been
		built on Windows but there is no reason it could not be. The code is
		trivial.

		This project is not mentioned in the makefiles because it was never
		part of Shores of Hazeron. It was also never needed in Windows because
		the sfx.har and glsl.har files were already built and in source control.

		To build it on Linux...

			cd hashpack
			reqt
			make

	shell.scripts - Helpful scripts that could be out of date but their 
		contents might show how some things are done, in general.

Databases
---------
	3 SQL databases are used by the servers; they can be hosted on one
	PostgreSQL server. The game client does not talk to the SQL servers.

	auLogin - Database of login accounts and their associated avatar ids. Also
		contains table of buddies and blocked ids. Created using initlogin.sql.
	
	auMail - Database of ingame mail awaiting delivery.

	auUniverse - Database of spacecraft/building/item designs, exchanges,
		stories, empires, and atoms of the universe.

		Before running for the first time, the initstatic.sql file is used to
		create the items data in the universe database.

		When the master server is first run, absense of a universe atom causes
		it creates the universe and 21 galaxies.

		When an avatar is created their initial home solar system is located.
		It may be created at this time, including the 3x3x3 box of sectors
		surrounding it, as needed.

		The client program creating the new avatar is redirected to the worker
		server that ends up hosting their new solar system.

		The first avatar to be created in the universe will find there are no
		building or spacecraft designs in the exchanges.

		Using the Agent window, an admin level avatar must then seed the
		database with the following data. The Agent window enables an avatar to
		enter a design studio from anywhere. This is needed to finalize and
		publish designs.

		Database ids of finalized designs are automatically generated. When an
		agent finalizes a design, the id can be specifically assigned. Note that
		if you overwrite a published design, atoms in the universe using that
		design will likely be broken.
			Building designs
				All designs in Blueprints/Buildings must be finalized and
				published in the building exchanged.

			Spacecraft designs
				All designs in Blueprints/Spacecraft must be finalized and
				published in the spacecraft exchanged.

			Fence and Item designs are already installed. Those .SoH files are
				loaded from program resource .qrc files.

			Pirate building and ship designs
				All designs in Blueprints/Pirates must be finalized but not
				published.

				Use the Agent window to configure the pirate empires with the
				spacecraft and building designs they need. A flag graphic is
				also assigned. They may also need some DNA.

				Once a pirate empire has enough data, it becomes active in the
				game. There is currently enough data to enable the Ogar and
				Seledon pirate empires.

				A story script expects design 2007 to be OgarShipMilitary.SoH.
				Be sure to change the austoryres/stories/8.Beacon/script.sh
				file if that changes. Look for worldCreateShip.

